    "q-88286": {
      "dom_id": "step-q88286",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$",
      "worked_solution": "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the $2$nd column.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\cdot 2(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1-2)\\cdot 2(1+2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
